WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.360
Every year, people spend about $8.5
billion on treatments for hair loss.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.120
It's an incredible figure,

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.880
and it speaks to how deeply
people are affected by it,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.240
and how badly they would
like for there to be a cure.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.440
But the thing is,

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.520
there's no treatment that actually
regrows large amounts of hair.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.640
A new treatment for baldness
may be on the way.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.720
I'm interested in the
subject, obviously.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.200
The drug was applied regularly
and new hair began to sprout.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.840
A cure for baldness might be
the most perpetually delayed

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.960
of all the medical advances we're
told are just around the corner.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.360
Now there are a few prospective
treatments that are very promising,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.800
and we can get to those in a minute.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.520
But first, it's good to examine
why exactly

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.200
hair is such a tricky problem

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.000
and how, over time, hype has often
played a bigger role than science

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.960
in getting people's
hopes up for a cure.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.640
So the first thing is hair
itself is pretty simple,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.640
it's just dead cells
stuffed with a protein.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.640
But the follicles that make hair
are incredibly complex.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.120
They're technically an organ
like your heart or your kidneys.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.680
They're very specialised structures

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.640
that form early in your development
and they can never be regrown.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.040
By the 22nd week,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.920
a foetus already has all
five million of the hair follicles

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.240
it will ever produce,

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.120
and the hair that these
follicles make can change,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.320
like how it gets thicker
and darker during puberty.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.560
But you can never grow new follicles.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.040
Now in terms of treatments, even
back in the 1950s, 60s and 70s

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.680
when we didn't know very
much about how hair worked,

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.480
cures for baldness
were still promised

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.040
and they were mostly
totally ridiculous.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.280
50 guineas for 15 grafts.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.040
Hides some of the baldness.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:37.960
And does it?

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:38.880
It does, yes.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.160
Experiments carried out on monkeys
are said to be encouraging.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.320
Testosterone injections, or have
steroids rubbed on your scalp,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.160
you could have electric shocks,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.840
or vacuuming on the skin where
the hair was supposed to grow.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.080
There was even a Japanese
pharmaceutical company

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.640
that marketed a CD of music that
was supposed to promote hair growth.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.400
There were so many fraudulent
cures that, in the mid-1980s,

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.040
the American Food
and Drug Administration

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.440
actually banned any medication
that claimed to treat hair loss.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.680
Now some real treatments
were actually found

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.320
towards the end of the 1980s,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.400
but the thing was, they came
about almost entirely by accident.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.480
Experiments which may produce
the world's first real baldness cure.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.160
It happened by accident.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.680
Doctors administering a new
drug for treating patients

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.000
with high blood pressure
found interesting side effects.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.880
A side effect of that medication
was it grew hair on people.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.600
It was a nuisance. It grew bushy
eyebrows and heavy body hair.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.600
And then a drug for enlarged
prostate called Finasteride

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.160
was shown to slow down
or halt hair loss in some people.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.120
The drugs didn't actually
regrow full heads of hair,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.720
and they didn't work
for everybody,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.160
but they were incredibly
big news at the time.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.160
The media referred to them as cures

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.440
and I think people thought
that they were the first step

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.400
in a new era of
successful treatments.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:51.840
The problem was

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.360
that they didn't follow from any
big revelation in medical science.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.560
Basically, we have
a treatment for a process

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.920
that we don't fully understand.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.800
And incredibly, in the last 30 years,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.040
those are the only two drugs

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.640
that have ever been
approved for hair loss.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.160
Since the 1990s, even though
we haven't found any new drugs,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.200
we've learned a lot about
how hair actually works.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.400
It turns out that,
unlike other organs,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.480
hair follicles are constantly
remodelling themselves,

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.000
changing structure depending on
whether they're growing hair,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.840
shedding hair or resting.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.000
And they use stem cells to do this.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.360
It's sort of like they're
constantly in development.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.640
And they do use a lot of the
same pathways and signals

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.360
that are used in early
human development.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.360
There's a biotech company run
by a Turkish-American billionaire

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.760
that has a very secretive drug

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.760
that works on one of these pathways,
that's currently in human trials.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.320
They say that it can regrow hair.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.360
And there are several
Japanese research groups

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.680
that say that they can
clone your hair follicles,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.960
grow them up in a dish,

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.320
and manipulate their
signalling to grow new hair.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.960
And then presumably transplant
it back on your head.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.040
And their work is also
in human trials.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.880
All of these discoveries have
launched massive new projects

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.600
that make better scientific sense
than previous treatments.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.600
But they're often in fields
that are relatively new

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.280
and quite complex themselves.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.080
Stem cells are historically
quite over-hyped.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.520
And there's still a lot we don't know

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.320
about the science
behind hair loss itself.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.520
So while a cure looks more realistic
than at any time in the recent past,

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.080
expectations are probably
still way too high.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.800
Thanks for watching. Don't forget to subscribe

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.640
and click the bell to receive notifications for new videos.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:23.880
See you again soon.

